00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:53.780
So basically what we're
going to look today is that--

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:57.450
we're going to look
at how can we predict

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:58.700
the failure of those machines.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.700
Because cost of failure
is actually quite large.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.880
A single electrical
subsystem failure,

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.780
and we focused on
that today, actually

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:09.920
costs anywhere between $100,000
to several million to fix.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.660
And the cost comes
from two things.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:16.520
One of them is the cost of
repair, like a direct repair,

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.250
and the earlier we can
spot the potential problem

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:23.870
and initiate the repair the
less, usually, this repair

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:24.470
costs.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.480
But the biggest
cost actually comes

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.430
from the truck being idle.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.200
Because in a
resource-constrained

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.980
environment, when the
truck doesn't hold coal,

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.820
it doesn't hold stone, rock,
we don't get any new revenue.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.940
That's where the biggest
cost coming from.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:44.780
So predicting failure allows
us to do this maintenance

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.240
and repair at a time when
we're not resource-constrained.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:50.750
So we reduce or eliminate
its idling cost.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:52.050
So again, what do we need?

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:53.780
We need an early warning, right?

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.880
We need an early
warning and ideally we

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:58.550
want to know how much time until
the truck is going to fail.

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:01.410
That would be our nirvana.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.650
Or depending on some
other types of failure

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:05.630
we want to know a level
of under-performance.

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:09.500
How deteriorated the
resource or a component

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.630
is so we can decide
which ones to take

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.210
for the proactive
maintenance first.

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:18.350
They estimated that
if you can predict

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.500
high voltage electrical
subsystem failures then

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:24.140
they could save up to
$4 million a year just

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:26.060
with this failure alone.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.010
And it's a scoping
on a three mining

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:31.200
site with 108 trucks like this.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.890
So now we have a kind of
problem and we have a need.

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:36.530
And we always try to be very
disciplined in establishing

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.960
those first before we're
moving towards machine learning

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.720
solutions because it allows
us to have a full circle

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.420
and estimate ROI of
our work as well.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.030
Now, what to date
we are working with.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:54.513
There's several dataset inputs
that we're going to work with--

00:04:54.513 --> 00:04:55.930
datasets we're
going to work with.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:57.720
But one of them is
that, hey, where

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.103
do we get predictive data
in order to spot the things,

00:05:01.103 --> 00:05:02.520
in order to spot
the patterns that

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:06.960
indicate a pending failure?

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.780
Those trucks are
equipped with sensors

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:13.140
that send the readings
as time series data

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:14.940
as well as alerts
and events which

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:18.090
are generated on those
trucks from actuators

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:20.760
from the smart systems
embedded in those as well.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.010
There's some dispatch data
and some other data sources

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:24.100
available as well.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.767
But ultimately, we're
going to work only

00:05:25.767 --> 00:05:27.540
with the time series data here.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.900
And each truck generates about
two gigabytes of data per day

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.080
and hundreds of thousands
of data points per truck

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.160
per second approximately.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:39.912
So obviously this is where
our big data is coming from.

00:05:39.912 --> 00:05:42.120
We've been working with four
years of historical data

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.410
available to date, three mining
sites, more than 100 trucks,

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:48.840
millions of alerts,
billions of sensor readings.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.360
And it definitely must be
a machine learning problem,

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:51.860
right?

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.430
We have a large enough dataset.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.510
But, you know, what
do we do with this?

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:59.840
Before I go further, I want
to take a step back and share

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.980
with you our learning that
over the years of building AI

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:07.070
solutions we've always
tried to narrow down

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:10.070
our problem as a
supervised learning problem

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.620
because it allows us to
measure how well we're

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:13.910
doing historically.

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:18.360
It allows us to give convincing
insight to end users.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.990
It's a very actionable
result as opposed

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:23.180
to things like an anomaly
detection when you say,

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.160
something seems wrong
with this truck.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:25.880
So what?

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.850
There's thousands of components
of this truck, right?

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:31.837
Or tried to create
some clustering

00:06:31.837 --> 00:06:33.420
and figure out certain
patterns on it.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:35.660
There's so many patterns
but most of them

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:37.580
have nothing to do with
electrical failures

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:39.140
that you're going to look at.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:42.350
So as a result we always
try to narrow down

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:43.900
the problem in some
way and state it

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:45.500
as a supervised
learning problem when

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:49.293
we have a known actionable
outcome that we can predict

00:06:49.293 --> 00:06:51.710
with the model whether it's a
classification or regression

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:53.830
type of problem.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:57.180
Now, where do we get the labels?

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.880
Well there are historical
maintenance data

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:03.300
and a historical database of
the spare parts and so on.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:04.850
The trouble is that
some of this data

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.690
has not actually been digitized.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:11.460
So this is where their
maintenance analysts

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:15.030
had to go back for a few
years, go through all the data

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:17.910
and actually extract
some of their failure

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:22.290
and predictive or preventive
repair information

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:24.570
and supply us those.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.310
Now we had so much data.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:30.090
It's great, but what about
those failures themselves?

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:32.520
Well this is where
small data comes in.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.290
And the small data
in this case, we

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:38.370
have only really a few
dozen failure events.

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:40.500
There was 31 catastrophic
high voltage failures

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:44.430
over those 108 trucks but let's
study one that they could find.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:49.860
And a few dozen events that's
resulting in suspension

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.410
exchanges or
recharges, so-called,

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:54.270
when it's deteriorating
and then pump it up

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:55.920
so it works a little longer.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:59.500
And most of those actually
went undocumented at all.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.155
So as a result this is
the most important data

00:08:02.155 --> 00:08:03.780
that we normally take
into our projects

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:07.800
and this is the data that seems
to have the lowest quality.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:15.360
So we had not as much certainty
that some events are not

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:16.430
missing from there.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:19.200
So we have to be very
careful how we use that.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.710
Now does it smell like a
machine learning problem still?

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:25.140
When you only work
with dozens of events

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:30.360
it's very hard to build a
model on 30 events or so.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.559
So what do we do?

00:08:32.559 --> 00:08:34.730
Well in general, like
as we move forward,

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:36.919
as we have a business
need, as we understand

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:40.559
our data at least a little bit,
we need to frame the problem.

00:08:40.559 --> 00:08:42.952
As I said, usually we
always try to narrow down

00:08:42.952 --> 00:08:44.910
on supervised learning
which is as a regression

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:46.290
and classification.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:48.000
Then we'll need
to understand how

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.120
to produce reliable labels
if they're not available,

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:53.590
modeling techniques to use,
and work on the feature

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:54.090
engineering.

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:55.882
So those are the general
kind of directions

00:08:55.882 --> 00:08:56.975
that we're following.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:02.793
Let's say what we
want to predict is--

00:09:02.793 --> 00:09:03.960
let's start with an obvious.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:05.550
We just want to predict
time to failure.

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:06.460
This would be great.

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:10.002
We know exactly when,
according to the model,

00:09:10.002 --> 00:09:12.460
the resource is going to fail--
component is going to fail.

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:15.060
But it's really hard to
build that because there's

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:16.530
very few samples.

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:19.260
And other than just
training the model

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:22.173
to find out whether
failure is going to happen

00:09:22.173 --> 00:09:24.090
or not we now need to
distinguish whether it's

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:26.730
going to happen one day from
now, seven days from now,

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:28.000
or 20 days from now.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.990
Which is a significantly
harder problem to do

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.090
and some failure types
actually are not developing

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:35.340
in progressive fashion at all.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.680
So we won't be able
even to say whether it's

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:38.910
one day or seven days.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:44.580
As a result we had to kind of
go back and look at the problem

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:45.130
again.

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:46.547
And what we normally
do, we always

00:09:46.547 --> 00:09:50.600
try to look at the problem
from the eyes of their human

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:52.360
who is already doing
the work today.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:54.510
Ideally if that's possible.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:57.030
In this case, the Assets Health
Supervisors, what they do

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.160
is that many times
a day they look

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:01.830
at their dashboard, some kind
of heuristic rules and so on,

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:03.515
and make a guess.

00:10:03.515 --> 00:10:04.890
I think this thing
going to fail.

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:06.660
I'll need to take
for the maintenance.

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:09.900
But what they do ultimately is
they take observations and make

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:11.580
some kind of conclusions
using whatever

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:15.920
human insight and their
knowledge that they have.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:20.520
So that teaches us two things.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:23.070
It's great that we can now have
those observations many times

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:23.570
a day.

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:25.670
It's much more than
just 30 failures

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:28.430
we need to work with but we
don't have those observations

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:29.120
historically.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:33.800
The good news is we can simulate
them from the failure events

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:34.410
that we have.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.320
And I'll show how we do that.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:39.920
Another thing that we learned
is that it's actually not

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:42.260
really important to know
exactly when the failure is

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:43.230
going to happen.

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:47.030
It's enough to say that it's
going to happen soon enough.

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:48.740
And within soon enough
usually for them

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:50.180
needs to be a few days.

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:53.600
A day in advance, maybe a little
late but four days, seven days,

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:54.937
is awesome.

00:10:54.937 --> 00:10:56.020
So that's what we learned.

00:10:56.020 --> 00:11:00.350
Now how do you
reframe this problem?

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:04.960
This is one of the maybe
most innovative approaches,

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:08.990
the most creative thing we've
done during this project.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:10.730
We basically
transform our events

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:12.680
that we have from
the maintenance

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:15.765
analysts that they have provided
us into certain observations.

00:11:15.765 --> 00:11:16.640
So how do we do that?

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:22.180
Let me make sure--

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:23.350
all right.

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:27.640
This is a timeline
for each truck.

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:29.960
And there's some trucks
that had no failures at all

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.110
so this timeline would
be clean without events.

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:33.110
This is a failure event.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:36.790
And this is a
maintenance event when

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:40.330
staff decided to
took the truck out

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:43.340
because they thought there would
be a problem and they fixed it.

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:45.220
But all of those is
indicating for us

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:46.780
that there is something wrong.

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:48.280
So what we can do,
we work with them

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:53.800
in order to say all time in the
lifetime of a truck we're going

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:55.570
to split into certain ranges.

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:59.680
The range before the failure
of a certain length we're

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:02.080
going to call as a failure
pending range, or the failure

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:03.910
range, or a bad range.

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:06.610
This is where we're going to
make observations every day

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:08.890
a few times a day and
these observations

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:10.930
will be labeled as one.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:12.490
Which in our case
meaning a failure

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:16.040
pending within this
period of time,

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:17.800
however long it
is seven days, 10.

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:22.030
I think we ended up with
seven days in the end.

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:25.410
There's also some
period of time when

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:29.390
there may be still a signal
because the failure may

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:32.200
develop earlier but maybe not.

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:34.000
That's kind of an
area when there's

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:35.290
uncertainty in the data.

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:37.720
We're not sure whether there's
a strong signal or not.

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:40.990
So we decided to
mark those ranges

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:43.010
and exclude them from
taking observations.

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:45.190
So we have a much
cleaner delineation

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:48.200
between there's a
failure pending soon,

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:50.990
and there's a clean zone
when we don't expect failure.

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:53.440
For example, over
the next month or so.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:58.480
So as a result we basically
have those clean ranges

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:01.120
marked as the green
and then the red ranges

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:02.740
marked as failure pending.

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:04.650
And sometime after
the failure was

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:07.300
fixed, because the way that
we're generating labels,

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:11.410
all of your labels are data
features as you'll learn later,

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:14.380
we also excluded
from consideration.

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:17.620
What's important
here to remember

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:24.250
is that basically we simulated
human observations the same way

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:25.680
they tried to spot the problem.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:29.000
And at each of those
points in time,

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:31.120
we kind of make an
observation and take

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:35.488
the data we know by that time
from all time series data

00:13:35.488 --> 00:13:37.030
and generate some
features that we're

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:38.140
going to fit in the model.

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:39.520
And the model will
basically be trained

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:41.650
on whether its observation
is from a clean range

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:43.360
or from the failure
pending range.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:44.317
That's it.

00:13:44.317 --> 00:13:46.150
So what we have done
is that we've basically

00:13:46.150 --> 00:13:49.450
interpolated dozens
of rare events

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:52.625
into hundreds of
thousands of observations.

00:13:52.625 --> 00:13:54.250
And you know, thousands
of observations

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:57.700
is already something we can work
with and build the model on.

00:13:57.700 --> 00:14:00.820
Although still
not a huge amount.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:02.470
So you have to be quite careful.

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:07.370
So what do we do next?

00:14:07.370 --> 00:14:10.060
An obvious way of working
with time series data

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:14.470
as Paul will tell me, is like,
hey, we build an RNN model.

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:18.500
It's obviously designed to get
a sequence of either alerts

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:21.020
and sensor readings or
some aggregate of those.

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:24.500
And then make a
prediction based on those.

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:26.690
But we end up with a
huge feature space,

00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:30.613
like tens of thousands of points
only for a few thousand labels.

00:14:30.613 --> 00:14:32.030
So what we're going
to end up with

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:35.660
is what's kind of known as
the dimensionality curse

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:38.750
when we use a
too-complex feature

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:42.440
space or too-complex model.

00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:44.460
We can obviously train
the model on those

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:50.240
and it may show a great result
on training but on the testing

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:53.720
it will not generalize and
the error will be huge.

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:57.657
And it will basically definitely
learn certain patterns

00:14:57.657 --> 00:14:59.240
but there are so
many patterns in data

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:01.130
that we will most
likely learn something

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:03.110
else than electrical failures.

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:05.390
And that's definitely
what we've seen.

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:06.740
So how do we deal with it?

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:09.080
How do we deal with
relatively few samples

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:10.940
and a huge future space?

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:13.080
Well, to fix that
there's a few things.

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:15.800
One of them-- we need to
control our model complexity

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:19.110
and not go crazy with creating
complicated neural networks,

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:20.580
for example.

00:15:20.580 --> 00:15:23.510
And we also should try to
reduce our feature space.

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:25.190
We already tried to
simplify the problem

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:27.810
by going away from regression
to binary classification.

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:30.450
That's as simple as we can get.

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:32.210
So what do we do next?

00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:34.940
In order to reduce
the feature space,

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:36.543
we should be working
more creatively

00:15:36.543 --> 00:15:38.835
on the feature engineering
than just fill the sequence.

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:44.720
If we want to employ some of
the automated dimensionality

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:46.190
reduction techniques--
in this case

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:48.260
it didn't quite work
because the data is not

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:50.720
distributed normally.

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:54.290
But what we did is that we
worked with domain experts

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:59.810
and understood how we can
embed a temporal effect

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:04.040
into our features made
from sensors and alerts

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:07.850
without actually having to work
with a full sequence of tens

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:09.690
of thousands of data points.

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:12.740
So what we've done is
basically at each time

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:16.010
that we make observation we have
this historical data available

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:17.420
up to that point.

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:21.380
What we've done is that we
defined several sliding ranges

00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:23.660
of hours or days.

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:26.510
And some of them
even shifted back.

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:28.600
And we created an aggregate
from those alerts.

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:31.280
And the sensors
and alerts counts

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:33.950
of alerts within those ranges
worked actually the best

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:35.010
in this case.

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:40.730
And the sizes of those
ranges as well as the offset

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.920
is what we've actually
been tuning as part

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:44.380
of the hyperparameter tuning.

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:46.520
So we tune our feature
engineering parameters

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:49.700
into hyperparameters as well.

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:53.510
What we've also been
doing is that, when

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:57.080
I was mentioning on how
we created those ranges,

00:16:57.080 --> 00:17:00.540
the sizes of those ranges we
actually also made tunable.

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:02.990
So our hyperparameters
tuning iterations

00:17:02.990 --> 00:17:07.880
was not only on the model,
it was on a complete feature

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:11.240
engineering pipeline as well as
on a label production pipeline

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:14.990
so that we can tune
that and understand

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:18.619
what is going to work best when
we have a four days window,

00:17:18.619 --> 00:17:21.290
a seven days window,
and how far back we'll

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:25.940
need to consider pushing
this clean range as well.

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:28.430
If you got more
detailed questions,

00:17:28.430 --> 00:17:31.790
you'll find me at the booth
for the rest of the day.

00:17:31.790 --> 00:17:34.220
He'll come up here and
we can talk about it.

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:38.070
Because we're taking our feature
engineering parameters as well

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:41.410
as labeling parameters into--

00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:45.050
as part of our
hyperparameters tuning cycle

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:48.200
we need to be careful how we
do a split on the training

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:48.980
and testing.

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:52.460
What's important is that we
need to split in such a way

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:56.480
that the failure event
and the windows resulting

00:17:56.480 --> 00:18:00.970
from this failure event will
either be only in a training

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:03.340
set or in a testing set.

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:09.490
Because otherwise we may cheat
by basically artificially

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:13.300
tuning those labeling windows.

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:16.600
And we may also
introduce a problem

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:20.830
because nearby observations
from today and from yesterday

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:24.100
will include data from the
last maybe 10 days or 15 days

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:25.570
depending on those ranges.

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:27.070
They may be very
similar so we need

00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:30.550
to make sure that we don't
use them in the training

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:33.750
and testing because it is
kind of cheating in this case.

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:36.000
So we've been very careful
that when we split the data

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.920
we actually split it
by events before we

00:18:38.920 --> 00:18:42.220
start doing any transformations
and then in tuning.

00:18:42.220 --> 00:18:46.360
Very important not to
pollute it in this way.

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:49.757
Also, we have to take care of
not learning individual trucks.

00:18:49.757 --> 00:18:51.090
Because there were some trucks--

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:53.048
actually most trucks did
not have any failures.

00:18:53.048 --> 00:18:56.740
We had 108 trucks and
31 electrical failures

00:18:56.740 --> 00:18:59.170
and some of the trucks
failed more than once.

00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:00.850
So as a result, we
had to be careful

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:05.600
not to learn from the huge
amount of alerts and sensors.

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:08.290
The patterns that
are truck-specific.

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:13.240
So as a result, we had to
include trucks, obviously,

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:16.270
that had no failures and pick up
some of the clean observations

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:17.260
from them.

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:19.750
We need to include trucks
that never failed in training

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:22.580
but failed in testing set
so we cover those as well.

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:25.620
So those are the things
we need to think about.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:31.040
Of course, when building
models that generalize as well

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:37.210
also means that we're not
just spotting a pattern

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:39.883
we're not interested in, which
is actually learning the truck

00:19:39.883 --> 00:19:41.050
or learning the mining site.

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:42.717
For example, if it
ends up that one site

00:19:42.717 --> 00:19:45.770
had more failures than another.

00:19:45.770 --> 00:19:47.810
Now, how do we control
model complexity?

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:49.948
Well, if we stick
to a neural network

00:19:49.948 --> 00:19:52.240
then we'll just use highly
regularized neural networks.

00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:54.370
But it's quite a bit of
effort and the good news

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:56.490
is that there are
algorithms that

00:19:56.490 --> 00:20:00.220
are very robust on these
relatively small data sets

00:20:00.220 --> 00:20:02.013
with relatively high
number of features.

00:20:02.013 --> 00:20:03.680
You know, boosted
trees, random forests.

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:05.860
We actually ended up with a
random forest model in the end.

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:07.340
That worked really well for us.

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:09.870
They've worked out
really well for us.

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:13.310
And so that's basically how
we approach the modeling

00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:14.150
techniques.

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:19.280
Now, something to share is
that what we've also done

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:20.960
is that we applied
additional smoothing

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:22.320
on the output of the model.

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:26.760
So in this case, this is
basically observations.

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:28.280
This is time.

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:32.240
This is the model output,
which is basically--

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:34.550
we can simplify the
probability of the failure.

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:36.470
Although, it's
slightly different how

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:39.440
you treat this after
random forest as an output.

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:44.150
But for the end user that's
the easiest way to present it.

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:46.940
And it's a little spiky, right?

00:20:46.940 --> 00:20:50.690
As you see, this is where the
failure happened in the past.

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:53.300
So as you can see, the
model actually, some days

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:55.580
before, started to produce--

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:57.080
the probability
of failure started

00:20:57.080 --> 00:21:00.693
to grow based on the model.

00:21:00.693 --> 00:21:02.360
But it was a little
spiky so as a result

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:04.160
we decided to smooth it.

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:05.450
It's a better user experience.

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:08.240
As well, allows us to remove
some of the potential noise

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:11.310
in this from the model as well.

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:15.200
And as you can see, when
the failure was dealt

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:21.250
with the model prediction
started showing that

00:21:21.250 --> 00:21:23.680
the lower probability as well.

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:28.750
Now, how do we
choose the threshold

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:32.880
at which we make a decision
that it's likely pending failure

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:33.600
or not?

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:36.900
If we choose it very high
then we can miss the event

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:40.272
or miss the failure or get
notified very late for it.

00:21:40.272 --> 00:21:41.730
If we choose it
very low then we'll

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:45.240
have too many false positives
and engineers will constantly

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:48.240
need to make a decision to take
the truck out for maintenance.

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:51.450
So knowing what's the
average cost of failure

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:54.750
and knowing what
the maintenance--

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:57.600
what the check-up
costs, we basically

00:21:57.600 --> 00:22:02.370
could quickly build
a formula that

00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:06.480
will allow us to calculate
how much money we're

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:09.390
going to save based on the
different result in here.

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:16.110
So it turned out that the
optimal was around this 0.5.

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:20.410
When we put it in production,
we've took into account

00:22:20.410 --> 00:22:23.550
another aspect, a human
aspect of interpretation.

00:22:23.550 --> 00:22:25.500
And the human aspect is this.

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:27.600
Those Assets Health
Supervisors spend

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:31.870
their life or most of
their life doing this.

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:34.350
And model ultimately
will predict--

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:36.270
will make some false positives.

00:22:36.270 --> 00:22:38.775
Will indicate a failure when
there won't be one happening.

00:22:38.775 --> 00:22:40.650
So we want to make sure
at the very beginning

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:42.240
is that we give
them experience that

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:44.480
will make them more confident.

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:48.420
So as a result we'd rather
miss some failures sometimes

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:49.833
but make sure
that, when they see

00:22:49.833 --> 00:22:52.450
our load, when they see
the critical condition,

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:53.470
they do react.

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:56.700
So as a result we choose
it a little bit higher

00:22:56.700 --> 00:22:59.170
for them to be more
confident in it.

00:22:59.170 --> 00:23:02.190
And that worked actually
really, really well although we

00:23:02.190 --> 00:23:04.800
did miss some failures.

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:07.120
I'll go through the
results a little bit later.

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:09.328
Now of course, we've built
it all in the Google Cloud

00:23:09.328 --> 00:23:09.960
Platform.

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:13.350
This is kind of a simplified
way how we in the end

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:15.130
architected the
production system.

00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:17.400
But basically whenever
we built a system

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:21.570
like this, what we're trying
to do is we're trying to find

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:23.610
what's our integration
between our AI

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:25.770
solution with the
rest of the world?

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:28.380
How do we get the data and how
do we get predictive insights

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:30.950
into the customer's hand?

00:23:30.950 --> 00:23:33.180
Teck Resources has
been basically managing

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:36.450
a lot of this stuff on prem and
we've built it in the cloud.

00:23:36.450 --> 00:23:39.720
So as a result, of course,
we've set up a data ingestion

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:40.920
pipeline with them.

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:46.320
And actually later
on the dataflow

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:54.240
was introduced versus how we
worked here with Cloud Composer

00:23:54.240 --> 00:23:55.920
and Airflow.

00:23:55.920 --> 00:23:59.040
But in the end we've
basically been landing data

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:02.290
in the cloud storage, which is
a very common way of doing that.

00:24:02.290 --> 00:24:06.270
And then we had a regular job
ingesting the data in BigQuery

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:09.722
as well as updating some of
the aggregates in the Bigtable.

00:24:09.722 --> 00:24:11.180
And the reason
we've chose Bigtable

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:15.000
is because it's very
good for sparse data,

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:18.210
like tens of thousands
of columns of sparse data

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:19.270
that we needed.

00:24:19.270 --> 00:24:21.160
And it was very useful
for us to pre-create

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:22.410
a lot of different aggregates.

00:24:22.410 --> 00:24:24.480
There's a lot of different
averaging windows

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.020
and a lot of
different parameters.

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:29.400
So we can then quickly sample
observations out of them

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:32.370
based on whatever
labeling techniques

00:24:32.370 --> 00:24:35.940
we're using and sampling
different aggregation windows.

00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:38.340
So we could iterate
through those

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:42.890
very quickly without
recalculating those aggregates

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:43.470
every time.

00:24:43.470 --> 00:24:45.660
So that's why we used
Bigtable for this, which

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:48.220
is kind of unusual in a way.

00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:51.780
And in the end we've developed
an API that basically sits

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:56.700
in the App Engine and behind
the Google Cloud Endpoint,

00:24:56.700 --> 00:24:59.190
have a REST Endpoint
for them to call.

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:01.500
So all they need to do after
all of this implemented

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:05.730
is basically tell me at
this point in time what's

00:25:05.730 --> 00:25:07.630
your prediction that
this truck will fail.

00:25:07.630 --> 00:25:10.710
Or give me predictions
for all 108 trucks

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:13.838
today or as of five days ago.

00:25:13.838 --> 00:25:15.630
That's basically the
API that we gave them.

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:19.740
The API behind the scene
calculated several--

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:21.120
made several
predictions, applied

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:24.090
the smoothing that we need, and
threw back out the probability

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:26.880
as well as whether
it's a load condition

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:29.840
or not based on a
selected threshold.

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:34.510
And API basically
picks the data from--

00:25:34.510 --> 00:25:36.810
picks the features
from Bigtable,

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:38.940
which are updated as part
of the data ingestion.

00:25:38.940 --> 00:25:43.590
It picks the model from
the Cloud Storage bucket

00:25:43.590 --> 00:25:46.050
so that we can update
model without impacting

00:25:46.050 --> 00:25:51.740
and restarting our applications,
and presents the result

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:53.620
as a JSON in the response.

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:54.940
So it's not that complicated.

00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:57.120
So all they need
to do is regularly

00:25:57.120 --> 00:26:00.510
call it and get the
results in front of users

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:02.178
or store them in
their own systems.

00:26:02.178 --> 00:26:03.720
And it's very
important that we could

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:05.610
embed it in their own
systems rather than give them

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:06.210
something new.

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:07.585
Because Assets
Health Supervisors

00:26:07.585 --> 00:26:10.500
already have a lot of things
and dashboards to look at.

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:13.230
They don't need
yet another thing.

00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:15.690
Although it may be
somewhat ugly-ish,

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:18.112
you can argue, it's really
in front of the users

00:26:18.112 --> 00:26:20.320
and what they're using now
and that's very important.

00:26:20.320 --> 00:26:22.440
And when we build the
systems with an API--

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:25.770
that's exposing an
API, that allows

00:26:25.770 --> 00:26:27.240
us this ease of embedding.

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:28.890
Ease of integration,
like as opposed

00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:33.420
to have very tight, very,
very, very tight integration.

00:26:33.420 --> 00:26:35.070
And as we build it
on Google Cloud,

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:37.722
we have all the operational
metrics in the Stackdriver

00:26:37.722 --> 00:26:39.180
and have a dashboard
that's showing

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:41.910
us exactly what's going on,
how much resources used,

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:45.490
how many predictions are being
made, and et cetera, et cetera.

00:26:45.490 --> 00:26:48.030
So very kind of sleek
and very easy to use.

00:26:48.030 --> 00:26:54.040
And it's been running basically
on its own with one failure,

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:57.070
in about a year and
a half, I think.

00:26:57.070 --> 00:27:03.185
So something got full somewhere
and that's why it failed.

00:27:03.185 --> 00:27:04.310
It wasn't monitored before.

00:27:07.090 --> 00:27:09.070
Let's look at a
specific example.

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:12.260
One of the failures,
how it was predicted.

00:27:12.260 --> 00:27:14.710
So this isn't a cold AIDE.

00:27:14.710 --> 00:27:18.010
It triggered a warning
on Thursday morning.

00:27:18.010 --> 00:27:19.720
The Assets Health
Supervisors made

00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:25.215
a decision in whatever way they
made that since it's legitimate

00:27:25.215 --> 00:27:26.340
let's take for maintenance.

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:27.423
They took for maintenance.

00:27:27.423 --> 00:27:33.340
They found this RP1 Contactor
that been failing, replaced it.

00:27:33.340 --> 00:27:36.460
But the predictive
model still was

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:38.102
reporting a pending failure.

00:27:38.102 --> 00:27:40.060
So they decided to take
it back for maintenance

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:43.120
and then found another crack
in the critical component that

00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:46.000
normally results in a high
voltage system failure.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:48.060
Basically electrical
cabinet blow up is--

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:51.160
and they blow up in
a spectacular way

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:53.490
and it's expensive.

00:27:53.490 --> 00:27:54.740
So they were able to fix that.

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:59.650
And then the model kind of then
dropped down its prediction

00:27:59.650 --> 00:28:01.178
to a normal level over time.

00:28:01.178 --> 00:28:03.220
And it's interesting even
though we're trained it

00:28:03.220 --> 00:28:08.290
on a certain range, it turns
out that the model was often

00:28:08.290 --> 00:28:10.630
predicting it a little
bit before that range.

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:12.850
So having this
uncertainty window

00:28:12.850 --> 00:28:15.610
helped us a lot when we
are kind of in the middle.

00:28:15.610 --> 00:28:19.780
It gave a much stronger, much
more differentiated signal

00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:22.120
for the model to train on
which is very important for us

00:28:22.120 --> 00:28:27.130
because we have
very few samples.

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:30.760
Thousands of samples
is very, very little.

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:31.880
So what do we focus on?

00:28:31.880 --> 00:28:34.460
We focus on the
quality of our labels.

00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:37.130
We also would like to have a
good quality of the features,

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:39.380
but to some degree model
can deal with that noise.

00:28:39.380 --> 00:28:41.005
That's a job of
machine learning model,

00:28:41.005 --> 00:28:42.610
in order to deal
with that noise.

00:28:42.610 --> 00:28:46.150
Of course, we want to reduce
that complexity but our focus

00:28:46.150 --> 00:28:48.640
is always quality of labels.

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:54.870
This is something that's kind
of cool that they told us four

00:28:54.870 --> 00:28:56.700
months after what happened.

00:28:56.700 --> 00:29:00.220
And I read it because I think
it's amazing, amazing results.

00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:04.050
So the work we did with the
Komatsu 930E haul truck fleet

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:06.550
has saved us half
a million so far.

00:29:06.550 --> 00:29:09.340
So we put it in place
in April of last year

00:29:09.340 --> 00:29:11.410
and that was June of this year.

00:29:11.410 --> 00:29:14.750
And it has so far predicted
six electrical cabinet blowups

00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:17.070
because the model predicted six.

00:29:17.070 --> 00:29:19.910
Now, four of these
were actioned.

00:29:19.910 --> 00:29:21.830
Assets Health Supervisor
took a decision.

00:29:21.830 --> 00:29:23.100
Yep, good idea.

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:24.805
Two of them were not actioned.

00:29:24.805 --> 00:29:26.180
Assets Health
Supervisors thought

00:29:26.180 --> 00:29:29.810
that the model doesn't know what
it's doing and they missed it.

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:31.400
It caused a failure.

00:29:31.400 --> 00:29:33.380
One cabinet blowup was missed.

00:29:33.380 --> 00:29:35.100
So the model didn't predict it.

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:37.790
Whether because we chose
threshold a little too high,

00:29:37.790 --> 00:29:39.830
intentionally or
because there was just

00:29:39.830 --> 00:29:43.190
a failure that was so
rapidly developing that there

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:44.270
was no pattern for it.

00:29:44.270 --> 00:29:46.280
That's possible too.

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:49.420
They estimated that it was
$3 to $4 million per annum

00:29:49.420 --> 00:29:52.540
that they would save from
this use case alone per year.

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:56.850
So it's incredible ROI that
we were able to achieve.

00:29:56.850 --> 00:30:00.600
And Kal, the CIO,
yesterday evening told me,

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:04.852
you know how much we saved
this year alone with this?

00:30:04.852 --> 00:30:06.810
On the 10 truck failures
that they successfully

00:30:06.810 --> 00:30:10.150
predicted and avoided they saved
the $4 million this year alone.

00:30:10.150 --> 00:30:11.940
So it's actually
turned out even better

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:14.280
than they thought before.

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:15.600
The result was even better.

00:30:15.600 --> 00:30:20.010
So that was predictive
maintenance use case.

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:25.360
Again, working with the
structured data very few

00:30:25.360 --> 00:30:27.660
interested events and
kind of a creating way

00:30:27.660 --> 00:30:31.460
how we frame this problem as
a machine learning problem.

00:30:31.460 --> 00:30:35.160
So now I want to move
to a cricket game

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:37.530
analysis, which is pretty cool.

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:42.170
We worked with the English
and Wales Cricket Board.

00:30:42.170 --> 00:30:44.730
And it's basically a
single national governing

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:48.030
body for all cricket games
in England and Wales.

00:30:48.030 --> 00:30:51.720
That includes the top league
games as well as college games

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:53.500
and everything in between.

00:30:56.010 --> 00:31:00.840
One of their tedious jobs
that their staff is doing

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:03.390
is that the analysts
need to watch

00:31:03.390 --> 00:31:08.490
hours and hours of cricket
video in order to capture--

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:10.650
extract information
from the games,

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:14.010
what happens when the bowlers
deliver it, what type of shot

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:15.570
it is.

00:31:15.570 --> 00:31:17.900
And so on and all
the characteristics.

00:31:17.900 --> 00:31:19.480
So it takes a very long time.

00:31:19.480 --> 00:31:24.210
It's very manual
work so why don't we

00:31:24.210 --> 00:31:26.940
try to create a system
that will either simplify

00:31:26.940 --> 00:31:33.390
that or basically just analyze
and extract all those events?

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:35.820
Manual analysis of this
doesn't quite scale.

00:31:35.820 --> 00:31:38.970
They usually analyze
only the top tier events

00:31:38.970 --> 00:31:41.520
and the rest they don't
because they have just no time

00:31:41.520 --> 00:31:46.110
and it's not affordable to
invest those efforts in this.

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:49.320
And another problem is that
they have a group of people

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:51.018
and they're all kind
of inconsistent.

00:31:51.018 --> 00:31:52.560
They all kind of a
few seconds apart.

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:54.430
Sometimes they make
a mistake and so on.

00:31:54.430 --> 00:31:59.460
So they're actually not
very consistent in analyzing

00:31:59.460 --> 00:32:00.950
the games.

00:32:00.950 --> 00:32:02.790
Where do we start?

00:32:02.790 --> 00:32:05.420
Just like I skipped
that from the Teck story

00:32:05.420 --> 00:32:07.170
but normally when we
come in the beginning

00:32:07.170 --> 00:32:09.580
we'll try to make an initial
discover and assessment.

00:32:09.580 --> 00:32:11.430
What are the realms of possible
within the domain we're

00:32:11.430 --> 00:32:12.150
looking at?

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:13.720
What are the
potential use cases?

00:32:13.720 --> 00:32:16.830
And we basically build a mini
kind of risk reward matrix.

00:32:16.830 --> 00:32:19.410
We look at the
feasibility and the risks

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:24.480
of solving a specific use
case with machine learning.

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:26.400
And then we look at
the potential value

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:28.830
and we calculate
certain weighted number.

00:32:28.830 --> 00:32:31.080
And that gives us an
input to an executive

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:33.000
and the senior leadership
which of those we

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:35.230
need to focus on first.

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:36.770
So not very difficult
to interpret.

00:32:36.770 --> 00:32:38.890
It's very powerful.

00:32:38.890 --> 00:32:42.100
And it turned out that both
operation use cases basically

00:32:42.100 --> 00:32:43.240
was the most fundamental.

00:32:43.240 --> 00:32:44.530
And the reason it's
the most fundamental,

00:32:44.530 --> 00:32:47.050
is the most important, is that
before basically everything

00:32:47.050 --> 00:32:49.030
else needs to be
done analysts need

00:32:49.030 --> 00:32:51.760
to look through those
hours and hours of footage.

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:56.713
And a game over the five days
is about eight hours of game

00:32:56.713 --> 00:32:57.380
normally, right?

00:32:57.380 --> 00:33:01.000
Like this is our first class
test game-- international game.

00:33:01.000 --> 00:33:04.670
And there's hours of
video but most of the time

00:33:04.670 --> 00:33:06.570
is idle as people do nothing.

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:09.820
There's 10 seconds basically--
bowl deliver in the 10 seconds,

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:12.730
few seconds play, that people
are really interested in.

00:33:12.730 --> 00:33:14.800
So extracting those
out of all those hours

00:33:14.800 --> 00:33:17.940
will remove a huge amount
of overhead right away.

00:33:17.940 --> 00:33:19.690
So that's what we
wanted to focus on first

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:23.260
and there was a number of
ways how we could do that.

00:33:23.260 --> 00:33:27.340
So let's look at the data
that we're working with.

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:30.850
We kind of know the
need in some way.

00:33:30.850 --> 00:33:36.050
So here's our big data
again as we look at this.

00:33:36.050 --> 00:33:39.310
It's a petabyte of
unstructured video data.

00:33:39.310 --> 00:33:42.130
There's tens of
thousands of hours

00:33:42.130 --> 00:33:44.950
of HD video or standard
video, depending

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:47.440
on the game being captured.

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:51.130
And five days of
footage up to 40 hours,

00:33:51.130 --> 00:33:56.170
usually kind of in 20s, is
about 100 to 200 gigabytes

00:33:56.170 --> 00:33:57.130
worth of data.

00:33:57.130 --> 00:33:59.770
So it's quite a
bit to work with.

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:02.350
And other than
the normal footage

00:34:02.350 --> 00:34:04.270
there's also a hawk
eye footage available

00:34:04.270 --> 00:34:07.270
which is a special camera at
some of the stadiums equipped

00:34:07.270 --> 00:34:09.352
that's tracking the ball.

00:34:09.352 --> 00:34:10.810
So it's pretty
impressive what they

00:34:10.810 --> 00:34:13.719
do but we've been mostly
working with just the footage.

00:34:13.719 --> 00:34:16.665
The footage can be either
televised or non-televised.

00:34:16.665 --> 00:34:18.040
And non-televised
footage is done

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:22.120
by cameras installed by analysts
somewhere in a static location.

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:22.929
And that's it.

00:34:22.929 --> 00:34:24.471
And the televised
footage is what you

00:34:24.471 --> 00:34:26.080
see on the TV, which is cool.

00:34:26.080 --> 00:34:27.010
It has commentary.

00:34:27.010 --> 00:34:28.400
It has overlays.

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:30.489
So as a result, we have
some additional input

00:34:30.489 --> 00:34:33.370
other than what's happening
in the game itself.

00:34:33.370 --> 00:34:37.630
We have data from the overlay
that we could possibly use

00:34:37.630 --> 00:34:40.900
and we have an audio data which
we could also possibly use.

00:34:40.900 --> 00:34:45.188
And you will see how later
it actually helped us a lot.

00:34:45.188 --> 00:34:47.230
But basically what happens
is that as the cricket

00:34:47.230 --> 00:34:51.554
game is played, he's a
bowler that's running.

00:34:51.554 --> 00:34:52.429
It's called a run-up.

00:34:52.429 --> 00:34:55.535
So he runs with the ball then
he finishes and goes "poh!"

00:34:55.535 --> 00:34:57.410
I'm demonstrating really
bad for those of you

00:34:57.410 --> 00:34:59.180
who know cricket, right?

00:34:59.180 --> 00:34:59.920
I see your smile.

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:02.420
But that was-- basically, they
release the ball and then the

00:35:02.420 --> 00:35:06.980
[INAUDIBLE] here with a bat
like bang, hits or misses.

00:35:06.980 --> 00:35:10.100
But that's really a very
unique moment in the game

00:35:10.100 --> 00:35:10.980
that we want to spot.

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:12.990
It's a very unique
position of players

00:35:12.990 --> 00:35:14.930
to make very unique movements.

00:35:14.930 --> 00:35:17.620
And pretty much all
televised footages just kind

00:35:17.620 --> 00:35:22.250
of very specific scene how they
film it from behind as bowler

00:35:22.250 --> 00:35:24.710
running like this.

00:35:24.710 --> 00:35:27.500
So, obviously,
there must be a way

00:35:27.500 --> 00:35:30.230
to just spot those patterns.

00:35:37.200 --> 00:35:39.805
Now, what we have looked
at in the very beginning,

00:35:39.805 --> 00:35:41.430
or we could look in
the very beginning,

00:35:41.430 --> 00:35:43.022
is use some of the
pre-trained model.

00:35:43.022 --> 00:35:44.480
As we're working
with Google Cloud,

00:35:44.480 --> 00:35:46.890
there's quite a few
pre-trained models available.

00:35:46.890 --> 00:35:49.200
We look at the Cloud,
we do Intelligence API.

00:35:49.200 --> 00:35:51.480
It has the ability to
detect scene change so we

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:54.630
can use that as input in our
model to see when the shot is

00:35:54.630 --> 00:35:57.660
changing so it moves from one
to another, which potentially

00:35:57.660 --> 00:36:00.950
could help us before this
ball delivery happens

00:36:00.950 --> 00:36:02.072
as well some annotations.

00:36:02.072 --> 00:36:03.780
Turned out they're
not very useful for us

00:36:03.780 --> 00:36:05.670
because it basically
been always detecting

00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:09.330
players like a cricket game or
some other ball game and so on.

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:10.990
Like a stadium in
a lot of things,

00:36:10.990 --> 00:36:13.760
but not quite useful
for what we need.

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:16.440
The Cloud Vision API
also can annotate

00:36:16.440 --> 00:36:21.210
what it sees on the frame
and it has an OCR component.

00:36:21.210 --> 00:36:24.330
And OCR component could allow
us to look at those overlays

00:36:24.330 --> 00:36:25.830
and see when this
overlay changes.

00:36:25.830 --> 00:36:28.350
Because overlay changes after
the ball delivery happens,

00:36:28.350 --> 00:36:28.920
right?

00:36:28.920 --> 00:36:30.600
So that's an interesting
thing to know.

00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:33.240
And they could use
speech-to-text API

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:36.540
in order to parse commentary
and apply maybe some NLP to it.

00:36:36.540 --> 00:36:37.930
We ended up not doing that.

00:36:37.930 --> 00:36:40.700
But that was an avenue
that was available to us.

00:36:40.700 --> 00:36:45.990
Now again, remember that we
want to present and state

00:36:45.990 --> 00:36:49.380
the problem as a supervised
learning problem.

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:52.770
So it's very important if you
always narrow down into this.

00:36:52.770 --> 00:36:56.850
So here's the data that analysts
extract, like a subset of it.

00:36:56.850 --> 00:36:58.710
For each delivery
there is a line

00:36:58.710 --> 00:37:03.390
and it has information of who
is a batsman and a bowler, what

00:37:03.390 --> 00:37:07.260
type of shot it is, wicket
type, and the timestamp as well.

00:37:07.260 --> 00:37:10.380
Where the ball
landed, and so on.

00:37:10.380 --> 00:37:13.290
So that was manual,
tedious work.

00:37:13.290 --> 00:37:15.480
Unfortunately we could
not use this data

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:18.000
reliably simply
because the timestamps

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:20.070
that they had could
not be matched

00:37:20.070 --> 00:37:22.620
to offsets in the video
in a continuous video

00:37:22.620 --> 00:37:24.350
because this continuous video--

00:37:24.350 --> 00:37:27.880
first of all, it doesn't have
a concept of a timestamp.

00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:31.450
And it's also sometimes
fast forwarded and so on.

00:37:31.450 --> 00:37:33.060
So there was no
easy way in order

00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:35.940
to match those to the
locations in the video.

00:37:35.940 --> 00:37:37.670
Plus they were not very precise.

00:37:37.670 --> 00:37:39.930
It was always by
a few seconds off

00:37:39.930 --> 00:37:41.520
and we really need
to be focusing

00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:43.170
on those specific
seconds of the video.

00:37:43.170 --> 00:37:44.820
So there was a problem.

00:37:48.870 --> 00:37:51.390
Before going further I
want to kind of review

00:37:51.390 --> 00:37:55.190
what we need to solve
and how we can map it

00:37:55.190 --> 00:37:56.440
to a machine learning problem.

00:37:56.440 --> 00:37:58.940
So what we need is, we need
to take this long footage,

00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:01.710
cut it into small bits.

00:38:01.710 --> 00:38:04.080
Basically that means
we'll need offsets

00:38:04.080 --> 00:38:05.930
in the video that
will indicate where

00:38:05.930 --> 00:38:08.550
the ball delivery happened
within a few seconds'

00:38:08.550 --> 00:38:10.403
precision.

00:38:10.403 --> 00:38:11.820
And then from there
on we can just

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:14.260
cut about even like
10 seconds frames

00:38:14.260 --> 00:38:16.590
which is 10 seconds fragment.

00:38:16.590 --> 00:38:21.240
This is usually long enough
for the game to be over.

00:38:21.240 --> 00:38:23.890
So we could also try
to train the model

00:38:23.890 --> 00:38:27.930
to find out when the ball--

00:38:27.930 --> 00:38:29.340
whether the play finished.

00:38:29.340 --> 00:38:31.050
But we didn't really
need to do that.

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:34.110
We needed to focus on
the very beginning.

00:38:34.110 --> 00:38:35.897
So the way that we
can kind of state it

00:38:35.897 --> 00:38:37.980
as a machine learning
problem is that we basically

00:38:37.980 --> 00:38:43.380
need to look at either
individual frames or a sequence

00:38:43.380 --> 00:38:44.190
of frames.

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:45.780
That's what the video is.

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:47.880
And then predict
whether it looks

00:38:47.880 --> 00:38:52.500
like this time in the beginning
of a ball delivery or not.

00:38:52.500 --> 00:38:55.740
It's quite specific,
again, compared

00:38:55.740 --> 00:38:58.380
to the rest of the recording,
the rest of the footage.

00:38:58.380 --> 00:39:00.000
So it must be doable, right?

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:02.100
But that's basically how
we stated this problem.

00:39:02.100 --> 00:39:04.360
So it's a
classification problem.

00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:11.100
We can potentially do it
multi-level classification

00:39:11.100 --> 00:39:13.680
by predicting whether it looks
like the bowler is actually

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:16.358
just doing a run-up
before the release,

00:39:16.358 --> 00:39:18.150
whether it's actually
the ball was released

00:39:18.150 --> 00:39:20.790
and traveling in the
air towards the batsman.

00:39:20.790 --> 00:39:22.810
But we didn't need it
to be that complicated.

00:39:22.810 --> 00:39:26.580
We just did it binary, whether
it's within this range or not.

00:39:29.370 --> 00:39:32.730
So either frames or
a sequence as I said.

00:39:32.730 --> 00:39:35.190
The bonus things
available is that, hey, we

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:38.490
can also look in some
heuristics from data structures

00:39:38.490 --> 00:39:41.190
from overlays as
well as use some text

00:39:41.190 --> 00:39:44.440
processing over the commentary
if we really have to.

00:39:44.440 --> 00:39:47.260
One of those things we
actually ended up using.

00:39:47.260 --> 00:39:49.410
So when we worked
with it last summer

00:39:49.410 --> 00:39:53.300
we first built a very quick
AutoML Vision prototype

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:55.200
when AutoML Vision
was still in alpha.

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:58.950
And it worked
really well for us.

00:39:58.950 --> 00:40:03.090
It told us that it's very
feasible to actually implement

00:40:03.090 --> 00:40:04.440
a proper system around this.

00:40:04.440 --> 00:40:08.230
What we've done is we've
just created a few--

00:40:08.230 --> 00:40:09.930
extracted a few
thousands of frames

00:40:09.930 --> 00:40:15.150
and we labeled them as either a
ball event or a non-ball event,

00:40:15.150 --> 00:40:18.150
which is everything else
that looks different.

00:40:18.150 --> 00:40:21.300
And from that alone
the AutoML model

00:40:21.300 --> 00:40:24.420
was able to achieve
quite a good performance

00:40:24.420 --> 00:40:29.340
and precision recall was
basically around 95% for that.

00:40:29.340 --> 00:40:35.340
So very quickly we could build
a model even just on the frames

00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:36.570
themselves.

00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:37.773
On the frames themselves.

00:40:37.773 --> 00:40:39.690
Now, there's replays,
there's some highlights,

00:40:39.690 --> 00:40:41.900
or sometimes they will
make a false positive.

00:40:41.900 --> 00:40:43.360
There are other
ways to handle it.

00:40:43.360 --> 00:40:44.777
But it gives us a
good confidence.

00:40:44.777 --> 00:40:46.140
OK, it's possible to do.

00:40:46.140 --> 00:40:48.540
We did some just very
quick manual labeling.

00:40:48.540 --> 00:40:51.390
So what we've done in order
to make it much more robust

00:40:51.390 --> 00:40:53.282
is that we needed more labels.

00:40:53.282 --> 00:40:54.990
The good thing is that
these days there's

00:40:54.990 --> 00:41:00.000
a lot of ways to actually
label images and label videos.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:03.060
There's many services you
can use, like crowdsource,

00:41:03.060 --> 00:41:05.430
those specialists.

00:41:05.430 --> 00:41:07.140
Instead of doing
that we've basically

00:41:07.140 --> 00:41:10.380
built a very simple interface
in order to label the videos

00:41:10.380 --> 00:41:12.840
and use some of the
research students

00:41:12.840 --> 00:41:17.652
that we crowdsourced to
do that for us to help us.

00:41:17.652 --> 00:41:19.860
But what we've also done is
a very interesting thing,

00:41:19.860 --> 00:41:22.680
is that we reduced
labeling efforts

00:41:22.680 --> 00:41:27.510
by using a few techniques that
allows us to approximately say

00:41:27.510 --> 00:41:30.990
where in the video there's
going to be a ball delivery.

00:41:30.990 --> 00:41:32.638
And what we use is two things.

00:41:32.638 --> 00:41:33.430
There's two things.

00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:35.940
One of those was
using Cloud Vision API

00:41:35.940 --> 00:41:41.230
OCR that detected where
there are overlay changes.

00:41:41.230 --> 00:41:43.020
And we've basically
built some heuristic

00:41:43.020 --> 00:41:47.200
around this that will say this
seems like a ball delivery.

00:41:47.200 --> 00:41:49.800
So that was pretty
good, but it was always

00:41:49.800 --> 00:41:51.630
late by some seconds.

00:41:51.630 --> 00:41:54.910
10, 15, 20 because
it happens later.

00:41:54.910 --> 00:41:57.540
And depending on the
broadcaster it's different.

00:41:57.540 --> 00:42:00.210
Overlay's different and the
time shift is different.

00:42:00.210 --> 00:42:01.940
Other things we
used is that we used

00:42:01.940 --> 00:42:04.410
a combination of unsupervised
learning, clustering,

00:42:04.410 --> 00:42:06.400
and basically transfer learning.

00:42:06.400 --> 00:42:08.330
So what we have done is
that-- and it's great,

00:42:08.330 --> 00:42:10.350
and we use those systems
available in our case

00:42:10.350 --> 00:42:11.400
ecosystem.

00:42:11.400 --> 00:42:14.340
But what we took is that
we took an Inception I3D

00:42:14.340 --> 00:42:20.040
model that was trained
on the kinetics data set.

00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:24.030
And it's about 500,000
videos if I remember,

00:42:24.030 --> 00:42:28.020
with various human
actions labeled in them.

00:42:28.020 --> 00:42:30.000
And that model was
trained on that.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:33.300
It takes an input
as a frame sequence

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:35.960
and it produces an
output which is basically

00:42:35.960 --> 00:42:37.592
a multilabel classifier.

00:42:37.592 --> 00:42:39.300
But what we've done
with it is that we've

00:42:39.300 --> 00:42:42.780
stripped the last
layers out of this model

00:42:42.780 --> 00:42:44.400
and then we ended
up with a fragment

00:42:44.400 --> 00:42:47.030
that's basically producing a
600 numeric representation.

00:42:47.030 --> 00:42:51.450
Like a 600 values
vector for each sequence

00:42:51.450 --> 00:42:52.470
that it's fed with.

00:42:52.470 --> 00:42:54.870
So if you can think of
it in the way of that we

00:42:54.870 --> 00:42:58.110
took a video sequence which
is hundreds by hundreds

00:42:58.110 --> 00:43:02.190
of page frame and we looked
at two seconds sequences.

00:43:02.190 --> 00:43:03.690
So it's 50 frames.

00:43:03.690 --> 00:43:07.010
So it's tens of
thousands of numbers

00:43:07.010 --> 00:43:10.100
times three for RGB channels.

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:14.340
So we took this and
narrowed it down to 600

00:43:14.340 --> 00:43:16.700
in such a way that it
came from a model that

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:21.140
was trained to recognize
certain human motions as opposed

00:43:21.140 --> 00:43:23.510
to in any other
random way or using

00:43:23.510 --> 00:43:26.030
some dimensionality reduction
technique or whatnot.

00:43:26.030 --> 00:43:28.100
So what we've done
after that is we'll

00:43:28.100 --> 00:43:30.410
basically run a simple
K-means clustering.

00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:36.560
And it became quite obvious
that within this video fragment

00:43:36.560 --> 00:43:39.110
that's close to
ball delivery there

00:43:39.110 --> 00:43:42.500
were certain clusters
showing up very quickly.

00:43:42.500 --> 00:43:44.090
So we use this
clustering technique

00:43:44.090 --> 00:43:46.145
and basically say, hey,
if the video sequence

00:43:46.145 --> 00:43:48.940
is in this cluster it means
it's close to ball delivery.

00:43:48.940 --> 00:43:52.490
It's actually worked reasonably
well to give us a general idea

00:43:52.490 --> 00:43:53.330
where we are.

00:43:53.330 --> 00:43:56.625
So again, quite an
interesting trick.

00:43:56.625 --> 00:43:58.500
And using this model
was super easy actually.

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:00.270
It's available on
the Tensorflow hub.

00:44:00.270 --> 00:44:05.350
So we basically just load it
and then modify it to our needs.

00:44:05.350 --> 00:44:07.910
Or it's actually available
in a GitHub as well.

00:44:07.910 --> 00:44:12.800
And the model was done
by Google's AI research

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:13.970
arm, DeepMind.

00:44:13.970 --> 00:44:17.360
Or Alphabet, I guess,
owners of DeepMind.

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:18.890
So a talented team.

00:44:18.890 --> 00:44:21.510
And they produce a lot
of things in open source.

00:44:21.510 --> 00:44:23.908
So what we've done
is that we've--

00:44:23.908 --> 00:44:24.950
how are we doing on time?

00:44:24.950 --> 00:44:29.120
Well, what we've done
with it is that we

00:44:29.120 --> 00:44:32.330
took those approximate locations
right here on the site,

00:44:32.330 --> 00:44:33.260
as you see.

00:44:33.260 --> 00:44:37.790
And we created a small HTML page
with basically a single page

00:44:37.790 --> 00:44:40.070
with some JavaScript
embedded in it in order

00:44:40.070 --> 00:44:43.490
to allow us to move second
by second, frame by frame.

00:44:43.490 --> 00:44:45.380
And very quickly mark--

00:44:45.380 --> 00:44:47.712
tag places in the video
as a certain event

00:44:47.712 --> 00:44:48.920
with the certain tags, right?

00:44:51.693 --> 00:44:53.360
We're actually just
going to open source

00:44:53.360 --> 00:44:56.130
it as available page so
everybody could use it.

00:44:56.130 --> 00:44:58.160
But basically we end
up with those tags.

00:44:58.160 --> 00:44:59.840
So we went through
the dozens and dozens

00:44:59.840 --> 00:45:03.140
of those long-form
footage, produced

00:45:03.140 --> 00:45:06.710
few thousands of those labels.

00:45:06.710 --> 00:45:10.820
And that basically split
our time of the footage

00:45:10.820 --> 00:45:14.270
into places where certain
elements of a game happen.

00:45:14.270 --> 00:45:17.730
And specifically, the time
when there's bowler running up.

00:45:17.730 --> 00:45:20.630
And that's throwing the ball
and the ball hit by a batsman.

00:45:20.630 --> 00:45:23.390
That's the thing that we
focused on and basically

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:28.490
made a binary classifier
out of it again.

00:45:28.490 --> 00:45:34.378
And what we've done with this
is that we trained two models.

00:45:34.378 --> 00:45:35.920
One of them is that
we basically took

00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:40.550
that kinetics dataset-based
I3D model which

00:45:40.550 --> 00:45:42.890
producing 600 vectors.

00:45:42.890 --> 00:45:45.500
And we use those 600
as the input features

00:45:45.500 --> 00:45:47.450
into a simple classifier.

00:45:47.450 --> 00:45:49.760
I mean, we could have just
stripped additional layer

00:45:49.760 --> 00:45:51.630
on top of it and make it not--

00:45:51.630 --> 00:45:54.470
freeze training of that bit.

00:45:54.470 --> 00:45:56.570
But in the end, in
any way you do it,

00:45:56.570 --> 00:45:59.990
we again used transfer learning
right because the model already

00:45:59.990 --> 00:46:01.103
trained on this.

00:46:01.103 --> 00:46:03.020
And we turn it into the
classification problem

00:46:03.020 --> 00:46:06.650
as opposed to
semi-usable clustering.

00:46:06.650 --> 00:46:08.240
And we achieved
very high precision

00:46:08.240 --> 00:46:12.230
and recall pretty quickly on
this in the very high 90s.

00:46:12.230 --> 00:46:14.630
And we also built
the frame classifier.

00:46:14.630 --> 00:46:17.990
We could have arguably
taken the same approach

00:46:17.990 --> 00:46:22.880
by possibly taking
the preexisting image

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:27.230
classification algorithm,
pick important layers from it,

00:46:27.230 --> 00:46:29.360
and then retrain it
for what we need.

00:46:29.360 --> 00:46:31.950
Fortunately this
product already exists.

00:46:31.950 --> 00:46:35.000
And it's called Google
Cloud AutoML Vision.

00:46:35.000 --> 00:46:36.980
Because that's
exactly what it is.

00:46:36.980 --> 00:46:39.620
So it's already pre-trained
on a lot of the photos

00:46:39.620 --> 00:46:42.650
and we just need to additionally
train it on the frames

00:46:42.650 --> 00:46:43.250
that we need.

00:46:47.020 --> 00:46:49.390
And we basically merged
those models together

00:46:49.390 --> 00:46:53.080
and made a decision and built
a small microservice API.

00:46:56.920 --> 00:47:01.120
Because it takes long time to
go through several hours of HD

00:47:01.120 --> 00:47:04.600
video and undo it and extract
frames and apply those models,

00:47:04.600 --> 00:47:06.130
it needs to be asynchronous.

00:47:06.130 --> 00:47:08.190
But again, we were
always thinking of it

00:47:08.190 --> 00:47:11.020
as an API that can be
embedded in other systems.

00:47:11.020 --> 00:47:14.710
So their API here is
that sending a message

00:47:14.710 --> 00:47:17.020
and it pub/sub
topic that gives us

00:47:17.020 --> 00:47:21.580
location of the video
in our Cloud Storage.

00:47:21.580 --> 00:47:23.512
There's a Kubernetes
cluster that runs workers

00:47:23.512 --> 00:47:25.720
that auto-scales scales
based on a number of messages

00:47:25.720 --> 00:47:28.780
in a queue that
picks up that video,

00:47:28.780 --> 00:47:32.650
goes through that with
OpenCV, extracts the frames,

00:47:32.650 --> 00:47:39.460
and feed those frames into
the kinetics-based model.

00:47:39.460 --> 00:47:41.680
And as well as send
some of the frames

00:47:41.680 --> 00:47:48.740
into the Google AutoML
Vision and then stitches,

00:47:48.740 --> 00:47:50.240
quote unquote,
"those two together."

00:47:50.240 --> 00:47:53.540
Basically build a simple
ensemble out of those

00:47:53.540 --> 00:48:00.470
and creates a JSON file that it
puts in another Cloud pub/sub

00:48:00.470 --> 00:48:01.470
topic.

00:48:01.470 --> 00:48:07.670
So how this is embedded is
that we send the message when

00:48:07.670 --> 00:48:08.648
the video is available.

00:48:08.648 --> 00:48:11.190
Or we can automatically send
the message in the pub/sub queue

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:12.400
when the video is uploaded.

00:48:12.400 --> 00:48:14.360
For example there are
many ways we can do it

00:48:14.360 --> 00:48:15.950
in a Google Cloud Platform.

00:48:15.950 --> 00:48:20.030
And then we just wait in a queue
for the producing to finish

00:48:20.030 --> 00:48:21.770
and we do whatever
we need to do.

00:48:21.770 --> 00:48:24.650
In the beginning, we
just created a small UI

00:48:24.650 --> 00:48:27.960
that will basically, in a
similar fashion that I show you

00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:31.825
with the labeling, it would
kind of show us the label so

00:48:31.825 --> 00:48:33.200
that analysts
could go through it

00:48:33.200 --> 00:48:36.560
and evaluate how
well it's working.

00:48:36.560 --> 00:48:39.530
But another not shown
here is that component

00:48:39.530 --> 00:48:43.220
that basically picks the
JSON from a pub/sub when

00:48:43.220 --> 00:48:46.550
the labels become available
and basically cuts

00:48:46.550 --> 00:48:49.310
the images into small bits.

00:48:49.310 --> 00:48:50.750
Very, very easy to do.

00:48:50.750 --> 00:48:53.900
So we have a full pipeline
by taking a long-form video

00:48:53.900 --> 00:48:56.210
and transforming
it in a short-form

00:48:56.210 --> 00:48:57.680
and it works pretty slick.

00:49:04.870 --> 00:49:06.730
When I was preparing
for it I was saying,

00:49:06.730 --> 00:49:09.850
it would be really cool that
Google introduced AutoML Video

00:49:09.850 --> 00:49:11.950
because it just made sense
because it would just

00:49:11.950 --> 00:49:14.620
do it in exactly the same way.

00:49:14.620 --> 00:49:15.520
Guess what?

00:49:15.520 --> 00:49:19.630
This morning it was announced
that AutoML Video Intelligence

00:49:19.630 --> 00:49:23.530
is basically the same
thing as Vision AutoML

00:49:23.530 --> 00:49:26.480
but works on a sequence of
video in exactly the same way.

00:49:26.480 --> 00:49:30.500
So what we could have done
if it was available earlier

00:49:30.500 --> 00:49:32.200
is that we could have used that.

00:49:32.200 --> 00:49:35.320
Now, maybe it will
work good enough

00:49:35.320 --> 00:49:38.170
on the videos that focused
on people's movement.

00:49:38.170 --> 00:49:39.007
Maybe not.

00:49:39.007 --> 00:49:40.840
It still remains to be
seen because kinetics

00:49:40.840 --> 00:49:45.970
is one that's been focused on
predicting the people movement.

00:49:45.970 --> 00:49:47.410
What people do
and their actions.

00:49:47.410 --> 00:49:50.800
So this domain transfer
worked really well for us.

00:49:50.800 --> 00:49:52.970
The transfer learning
worked really well for us

00:49:52.970 --> 00:49:54.970
but maybe it's too generic.

00:49:54.970 --> 00:49:56.680
But we will definitely
try it and I'm

00:49:56.680 --> 00:49:59.320
pretty sure we'll get
some good initial results.

00:49:59.320 --> 00:50:01.630
So it's great that we have
availability of those,

00:50:01.630 --> 00:50:04.388
even if we don't end up
using them 100% at the end,

00:50:04.388 --> 00:50:06.430
it will already give us
a very quick idea if it's

00:50:06.430 --> 00:50:09.040
possible or not and we can
go through more complicated

00:50:09.040 --> 00:50:10.990
solutions as needed.

00:50:10.990 --> 00:50:14.340
[ELECTRONIC MUSIC]

